Smart solid materials with special surface wettability are actively being sought owing to their diverse potential applications. 1-2 Super-hydrophobic porous materials is of great interest since both the water-repellent surface and inherent porosity can be provided, which enable them to be used for some special applications such as contaminated water treatments and fuel purification.3-4
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed from metal-oxo nodes interconnected by poly-dentate organic ligands is a kind of attractive crystalline porous materials which have shown great promise in various applications.5-13 Recent research progress has witnessed increasing attention in fabricating hydrophobic MOFs, not only to combat their failure in applications under humid conditions, but also endow them with improved or even novel functional properties.14-25 Benefiting from the designable and flexible features of MOFs, decorating MOFs with hydrophobic moieties has been widely used for imparting hydrophobicity into MOFs. Encouraging progress has been achieved in improving the water-repelling capacity of MOFs by functionalizing the organic ligands with hydrophobic groups.28-25 Surprisingly, there is no attempt to design the metal-oxo nodes for functional hydrophobic MOFs, in spite of their easily modified nature.26-31 In addition, the intrinsic presence of metal-OH groups at metal-oxo nodes of MOFs enables a facile and direct decoration process,30,32 showing advantages as compared with the ligand modification process that requires extra functional groups as “tags” for post modification steps.
Herein we initiated a challenging attempt to prepare superhydrophobic MOFs by metal-oxo nodes engineering. $N H_{2}-$ functionalized MOFs have attracted intensive interest due to their unique properties distinguished from bare MOFs. $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) represents one of the most appealing MOFs showing great potential in different applications and thus was selected as a proof-of-concept.28,31,33-36 Phenylsilane (PhSiH3) was grafted onto the Zr-O metal-oxo nodes of $N H_{2}-U i O{-}G6(Z r)$ by reacting with the Zr-OH groups, exposing the hydrophobic phenyl groups to form the super-hydrophobic $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) $(N H_{2}-U i O-66(Z r)-$ shp) with a high water contact angle of 161 ° (Scheme 1). The thus obtained $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp shows excellent base resistance and holds great promise in versatile applications including organic/water separation, self-cleaning and liquid marbles. This work demonstrates the first attempt of metal-oxo nodes engineering for super-hydrophobic MOFs. The precise modification on the metal-oxo nodes of MOFs without blocking the windows of pores maximizes the preservation of the inherent accessibility of pore textures, ensuring their unique superiority in practical applications. Thus, this work advances a novel and effective concept for designing super-hydrophobic MOFs for their practical uses.
The reaction between $N H_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ and phenylsilane was conducted in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ saturated acetonitrile with the presence of $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ , which has been reported effective for silane activation.37 The consistent XRD patterns of $N H_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ before and after reaction indicated the preservation of MOF structure (Fig. S1).
New FT-IR peaks corresponding to aromatic C-H (3072 and 3051 cm-1) and Si-O bonds (1130 and $1031~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ) appeared in the silylated $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr), suggesting the successful insertion of phenylsilane into $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr).38-39 The silylated $N H_{2}$ -UiO$66(Z\mathfrak{r})$ shows enhanced hydrophobicity with contact angles increased from $0~^{\circ}$ for pristine $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) to $88^{\circ}$ and $115^{\circ}$ for the samples prepared at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , respectively (Fig. S3).
Increasing the amount of PhSiH3 or using other hydrogen silanes such as 1,1,3,3-tetramethyldisiloxane (TMDS) or diphenylsilane (DPS) was not effective to further improve the hydrophobicity  (Fig. S4-S7). In contrast, the amount of $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ greatly affects the reaction and a highest water contact angle of 161 ° was obtained with $20\mathrm{mol}\%$ $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ used, indicative of the formation of super-hydrophobic MOFs (Fig. 1a and S8-9). It is worth noting that this contact angle value is higher than that of most reported hydrophobic MOFs (Table S1). The structural retention of the super-hydrophobic $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) (denoted as $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp) was confirmed by the well-preserved XRD patterns (Fig. 1b). Compared with $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr), which shows a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $974.4~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp displays a decreased but still high BET surface area of $656.5\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. S10), indicating the maintenance of the permanent porosity. The somewhat decreased BET surface area is within expectation and is a common phenomenon for MOFs after introduction of new constituents.40
The structure of the $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp was further studied by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum. The $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum of phenylsilane is greatly affected by HF, which induces the shift of the signals for $A r-H$ , disappearance of Si-H and a new signal at 7.35 ppm, probably attributed to benzene due to the dephenylation of phenylsilane (Fig. S11). Since HF is required for the digestion of Zr-MOFs for $^{1}\mathsf{H}$ NMR analyses, the $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum of phenylsilane obtained in HF/DMSO $({\mathsf{d}}_{6})$ was used for comparison.
The well matched $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectrum of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp with that of either $N H_{2}{\cdot}\cup i0–66(Z r)$ or phenylsilane indicated the grafting of phenylsilane in $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) (Fig. 1c), which was also proved by $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ cross-polarization magic-angle-spinning (CPMAS) NMR spectra, with the appearance of new signals corresponding to phenyl groups at around 152, 134 and 127 ppm (Fig. S12).
1H NMR spectra revealed that the amount of phenylsilane encapsulated in silylated $N H_{2}-U i O-66(Z r)$ varied with that of $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ , reaching a highest phenylsilane/linker ratio of 0.44 in $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp $(20\mathrm{\mol\%}$ of $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3})$ , suggesting the great dependence of the silylation efficiency on $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ (Fig. S13). In addition, the hydrophobicity of these silylated MOFs well matches with the amount of phenylsilane, evidencing that the improved hydrophobicity is truly induced by the encapsulated phenylsilane.
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images coupled with EDS mapping revealed the encapsulation of phenylsilane inside of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp, insteading of staying on the surface (Fig. S 14). Thus, both ligands and metal-oxo nodes should be the possible positions for phenylsilane to be interacted. To determine the reaction site of the phenylsilane, $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp was digested by HF/MeOH and the collected ligand from the liquid phase shows well matched $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and GC-MS spectra with pure ligand (H2ATA) and the ligand from digested $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr), excluding the possibility of reaction between the ligand and phenylsilane (Fig. S15-16).
Given that phenylsilane could easily react with hydroxyl groups,32 it is assumed that phenylsilane is grafted on the Zr-O metal-oxo nodes where the Zr-OH groups are present.30, 41 To verify our assumption, $^1\mathsf{H}$ CPMAS NMR analyses were performed. As shown in Fig. 1d, the $^1\mathsf{H}$ CPMAS NMR spectrum of $N H_{2}$ -UiO$66(Z\mathfrak{r})$ shows two main regions: the signal at low δ (1.2 ppm) for Zr-OH groups and the signals at high δ (7-8 ppm) for protons from the aromatic rings, in agreement with that of previous reports.42 The strong signal at around 2.7 ppm is probably due to the encapsulated solvents. After reacting with phenylsilane, the signal for Zr-OH groups (1.2 ppm) disappeared in $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp, indicating the consumption of Zr-OH to form Si-O bonds, which has already been confirmed by FT-IR spectra (Fig. S8). The quenching of Zr-OH and the presence of Si-O also rule out the existence of simply encapsulated phenylsilane in the pores. The grafting of phenylsilane on Zr-O nodes was further proved by the similar $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR spectra between phenylsilane and silylated $Z r O_{2}$ , which was prepared by replacing $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) with $Z r O_{2}$ in the material preparation process (Fig. 1c).
Based on the above results, a tentative mechanism for the formation of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp can be proposed. The reaction starts with the activation of Si-H by $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ as supported by the generation of bubbles, probably ${\sf H}_{2}$ , after mixing $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ and phenylsilane, which is consistent with a similar mechanism in the previous report.37 With the presence of $N H_{2}{\cdot}\cup i0{-}66(Z r)$ , the activated phenylsilane easily reacts with Zr-OH to form the Si-O bonds, exposing the hydrophobic phenyl groups to form the super-hydrophobic MOFs. The presence of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ presumably promotes the reaction since the product obtained under ${\sf N}_{2}$ shows an obviously lower water contact angle of $124^{\circ}$ (Fig. S17). This is probably due to the enhanced acidic condition in the presence of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , which promotes the dissolution of $\mathsf{C s}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ and thus improves the reaction efficiency.
In view of the importance of chemical stability for practical applications, the stability of $N H_{2}{-}\mathsf{U i O}{-}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ -shp under different conditions was tested. Although different kinds of stable MOFs have been developed recent years, they are usually fragile in basic aqueous solutions, which severely hampers their applications. XRD analyses revealed that the inherent acid resistance of $N H_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{U i O-}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ is well maintained in $N H_{2}$ -UiO66(Zr)-shp, as confirmed by its excellent stability in concentrated HCl (12 M) and $H N O_{3}$ (16 M) (Fig. 2a and Fig. S18). What is more interesting is that $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp also shows excellent durability with well preserved crystalline structure even after exposing to $0.1\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ solution for 5h, in contrast to the base labile $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) that decomposes within 10 min (Fig. 2a and Fig. S18). The robust viability of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp under these harsh conditions was further confirmed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms (Fig. 2b). The slight increase of the BET surface area for acid treated samples is probably due to the removal of some unknown coordination species encapsulated in the frameworks, which was also observed in the pristine $N H_{2}$ - UiO-66(Zr) and the previously reported MOFs (Fig. S19). 43-44 Since $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp can only float on the meniscus to obstruct the entry of OH- into the pores, no improvement of BET surface area was observed for base-treated sample (Fig. S20). FT-IR spectra and contact angle measurements give more evidences to the good stability of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp under these harsh conditions (Fig. S21-22). These results demonstrated that metal-oxo nodes engineering with phenylsilane is an effective way to generate highly stable super-hydrophobic MOFs with excellent acid/base resistance.
