Separation of organic compounds from contaminated water has become an environmentally important issue.45-46 Intrigued by the super-hydrophobicity of $N H_{2}-U i O-66(Z r)$ -shp, we investigated the selective sorption ability of $N H_{2}{\cdot}U i O{-}G6(Z r)$ -shp towards toluene, a model organic compound, from water. Owing to the superhydrophobicity, $N H_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ -shp pellet quickly adsorbed the Oil Blue N labeled toluene from water, resulting in immediate change of the pellet’s color from yellow to blue and the disappearance of the solution’s blue color around the pellet (Fig. S23a and Video S1). The fast adsorption of toluene by $N H_{2}$ -UiO$66(Z\mathfrak{r})$ -shp indicates the easy accessibility of the pore textures. In contrast, the hydrophilic $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr) shows poor adsorption ability with negligible color change of either the solution or pellet (Fig. S23b and Video S2). Actually, the $N H_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{U i O}{\cdot}66(Z\mathsf{r})$ pellet was quickly wetted by water, as also indicated by the good dispersion of the powder sample in water (Fig. S24). The amount of toluene
For continuously separating organics from water, $N H_{2}$ -UiO66(Zr)-shp was coated on a stainless mesh covered on a test tube with a window cut on its side, rendering a separator with a mesh window showing selective wettability (Fig. 4a and Fig. S26). When the separator was placed into the toluene contaminated water pool, the super-hydrophobic mesh allowed only organic phase to immediately flow through the window and entered the tube (Fig. 4b and Video S3). The toluene on the top of water was completely removed and simply pumped away from the test tube to realize a continuous separation process (Fig. 4b and S27). The $N H_{2}$ -UiO66(Zr)-shp coated mesh can also be applied to flow-assisted continuous separation of organic/water droplets. When toluene and water droplets were continuously dispensed alternatively onto the coated mesh, only toluene was collected into the vial, implying its potential for in-situ product purification during organic synthesis in a flow system (Fig. 4c, S28 and Video S4).
The excellent stability and high capacity of super-hydrophobic $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp to repel water promoted us to further explore its application in self-cleaning. $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp powder was drop-casted onto the surface of a glass, on which some dust particles were placed. Upon dropping water, the dust particles were rolled up by the water drops, which ran off the glass surface (Fig. S29 and Video S5). This result, accompanied with the high stability of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp, reinforces the utility of the material in extended applications under harsh environment such as strong acidic and basic conditions.
In addition to the applications in solid-state, $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)- shp is also applicable to aqueous solutions such as liquid marbles. Liquid marbles represent a new strategy for the manipulation of liquid droplets and have been attractive in microfluidics and sensing applications.47-49 A magnetic liquid marble was prepared by simply rolling an $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ containing water droplet on a pile of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp particles (Fig. 5a-b). The as-formed liquid marble behaves like a soft solid and shows high mechanical robustness to allow handling with a pair of tweezers, remain intact after being transferred onto a piece of paper, and allow a high speed rotation on water surface (Fig. 5c-e and Video S6). The motion of the liquid marble can be easily manipulated with an extra magnet (Video S7). These great features make it a very promising candidate for applications in microfluidic devices. Moreover, the porous $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp shell allows for easy diffusion of small molecules, indicating its potential for gas/vapor sensing. For a demonstration, ammonia sensing from an aqueous $N H_{3}$ -emitting source was carried out over a liquid marble encapsulating $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ solution. Upon adding $N H_{3}\cdot H_{2}O$ to the water pool where the liquid marble floated, rapid color change was observed within 1 min (Fig. S30). This fast sensing performance is comparable to the recently reported super-hydrophobic COFVF and superior to nonporous materials, indicative of its great promise in sensing applications.50
In summary, we have demonstrated a new strategy of metaloxo nodes engineering of MOFs with phenylsilane to achieve super-hydrophobic $N H_{2}-U i O-66(Z r)$ -shp with a high contact angle of 161 °. Highly improved base resistance is enabled by the ability of $N H_{2}$ -UiO-66(Zr)-shp to repel water, in contrast to $N H_{2}$ -UiO$66(Z\mathfrak{r})$ which quickly decomposes under basic condition. The super-hydrophobicity and excellent stability of $N H_{2}{\cdot}\cup i0–66(Z r)$ - shp also endow it with great promise in versatile applications, including organic/water separation, self-cleaning, and liquid marbles for microfluidic devices and sensing. Given that phenylsilane can be easily grafted on a hydroxyl surface and the widely presence of metal-OH groups in MOFs, our strategy of metal-oxo nodes decoration for super-hydrophobic MOFs can thus be extended to various MOFs to generate numerous superhydrophobic MOFs. Therefore, our research work not only provides a promising material for various functional applications, but more importantly also advances a new and general approach for designing super-hydrophobic MOFs for practical uses.
